Also, $d_{D-1}$ cannot be 0 because it's the MSD of a number $\ge 10$. So $d_{D-1} \in \{1, \dots, 9\}$.
If $d_{D-1} = k$, then each of the remaining $D-1$ digits can be any digit from $0$ to $k-1$.
The number of ways to choose the remaining digits is $k^{D-1}$.
