The problem asks us to simulate a process involving $N$ aliens over $N$ years.
Let $A_i$ be the initial number of stones the $i$-th alien has.
The $i$-th alien becomes an adult exactly $i$ years later.
When the $i$-th alien becomes an adult (which happens at year $i$), every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone to the $i$-th alien.
We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.

Let's denote the number of stones the $j$-th alien has at year $t$ as $C_j(t)$.
Initially, at year 0, $C_j(0) = A_j$ for all $j=1, \dots, N$.
At year $i$ (where $1 \le i \le N$), the $i$-th alien becomes an adult.
The rule says: "every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone as a congratulatory gift to the alien who has just become an adult."
Who are the adults at year $i$?
The aliens who become adults are $1, 2, \dots, N$.
Alien $k$ becomes an adult at year $k$.
So at year $i$, aliens $1, 2, \dots, i$ have become adults.
Wait, let's re-read carefully.
"The $i$-th alien ... will become an adult exactly $i$ years later."
"When someone becomes an adult ... every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone ... to the alien who has just become an adult."
So at year $i$, the $i$-th alien becomes an adult. The set of adults present at this moment includes aliens $1, 2, \dots, i$.
Wait, does alien $k$ ($k < i$) stay an adult? Yes, once an adult, presumably they remain an adult. The problem doesn't say they stop being adults.
So at year $i$, the set of adults is $\{1, 2, \dots, i\}$.
For each $k \in \{1, \dots, i\}$, if $C_k(i-1) \ge 1$, then alien $k$ gives 1 stone to alien $i$.
So, the number of stones alien $i$ receives at year $i$ is equal to the number of aliens $k \in \{1, \dots, i\}$ such that $C_k(i-1) \ge 1$.
Let $S_i$ be the number of stones alien $i$ receives at year $i$.
$S_i = \sum_{k=1}^i [C_k(i-1) \ge 1]$, where $[P]$ is 1 if predicate $P$ is true, 0 otherwise.
After receiving these stones, the number of stones alien $i$ has becomes $C_i(i) = C_i(i-1) + S_i$.
For $k \neq i$, $C_k(i) = C_k(i-1)$.
Wait, do the adults lose stones? Yes, "gives exactly one stone".
So if $C_k(i-1) \ge 1$, then $C_k(i) = C_k(i-1) - 1$.
If $C_k(i-1) = 0$, then $C_k(i) = C_k(i-1) = 0$.
So, for each year $i$ from 1 to $N$:
1. Calculate $S_i = \text{count of } k \in \{1, \dots, i\} \text{ such that } C_k(i-1) > 0$.
2. Update $C_i(i) = C_i(i-1) + S_i$.
3. For all $k \in \{1, \dots, i\}$, if $C_k(i-1) > 0$, update $C_k(i) = C_k(i-1) - 1$.

Let's trace Sample 1.
$N=4$, $A = [5, 0, 9, 3]$.
Initially $C = [5, 0, 9, 3]$.

Year 1: Alien 1 becomes adult.
Adults: $\{1\}$.
$C_1(0) = 5 > 0$. So alien 1 gives 1 stone to alien 1.
$S_1 = 1$.
$C_1(1) = 5 + 1 = 6$.
